# {{lb|en|of a|person|or|skin}} Lacking coloration ([[tan]]) from ultraviolet light; not [[tanned]].
#: {{syn|en|fair|pale}}
#: {{ant|en|tanned}}
# {{lb|en|of an|animal}} Affected by [[leucism]].
# {{lb|en|of [[coffee]] or [[tea]]}} [[contain|Containing]] [[cream]], [[milk]], or [[creamer]].
#: {{ant|en|black}}
# {{lb|en|board games|chess}} The standard denomination of the playing [[piece]]s of a [[board game]] deemed to belong to the white set, no matter what the actual colour.
#: {{ux|en|The '''white''' pieces in this set are in fact made of light green glass.}}
# Pertaining to an ecclesiastical order whose adherents dress in white [[habit]]s; [[Cistercian]].
# [[honourable|Honourable]], [[fair]]; [[decent]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|2|passage='''White''' as thy fame, and as thy honour clear.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1737|title=First Epistle of the Second Book of Horace|authorlink=Alexander Pope|first=Alexander|last=Pope|newversion=republished in|title2=The Complete Poetical Works of Alexander Pope|location2=Boston, New York|publisher2=Houghton, Mifflin and Company|year2=1902|page2=194|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/completepoetical00pope/page/194|passage=No '''whiter''' page than Addison's remains. / He from the taste obscene reclaims our youth, / And sets the passions on the side of Truth,}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Her Mountain Lover|year=1901| page=[http://books.google.com/books?id=N88SAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA51 51]| author={{w|Hamlin Garland}}| passage=“I’ll put you down at my club; and then, the governor will want to see you in the country.” / Jim had no idea of what was involved in being put down at a club, but he consented. “That ’s mighty '''white''' of you, old man, but I don’t know where I shall make down.”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1916|author=Julia Frankau|title=Twilight
|passage=He's a fine fellow, this Gabriel Stanton, a '''white''' man all through}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1953|author={{w|Raymond Chandler}}|title=The Long Goodbye|page=12|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2010
|passage=‘We've only met twice and you've been more than '''white''' to me both times.’}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=A.D.|issue=1|page=34| author={{w|United Church of Christ}}| year=1976| passage=Even decency has been regarded as a '''white''' or Christian attribute, as is evidenced by the expression "that's very '''white''' of you"}}
# Grey, as from old age; having silvery hair; [[hoary]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|act=III|scene=ii|passage=Your high engendered battles 'gainst a head / So old and '''white''' as this.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Characterized by freedom from that which disturbs, and the like; fortunate; happy; favourable.
#* {{RQ:Scott Guy Mannering|passage=On the whole, however, the dominie reckoned this as one of the '''white''' days of his life.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Regarded with especial favour; favourite; darling.
#* '''c. 1626''', {{w|John Ford (dramatist)|John Ford}}, ''{{w|Tis Pity She's a Whore}}''
#*: I am his '''white''' boy, and will not be gulled.
# {{lb|en|politics}} Pertaining to [[constitutional]] or anti-[[revolutionary]] [[political parties]] or movements.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1932|author={{w|Duff Cooper}}|title=Talleyrand|page=163|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2010
|passage=Aimée de Coigny had always adopted with enthusiasm the political views of her ruling lover and she had thus already held nearly every shade of opinion from red republicanism to '''white''' reaction.}}
# {{lb|en|of tea}} Made from immature leaves and shoots.
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Tea Enthusiast's Handbook|author=Mary Lou Heiss & Robert J. Heiss| year=2012| ISBN=1607743787| passage=Most often consisting of a budset pluck, a frost tea has the clarity and freshness of a '''white''' tea, with the richness and lingering finish of a finely crafted black tea.}}
