# {{lb|en|typography}} Said of a symbol or character [[outline]], not [[solid]], not [[filled]] with color. Compare {{m|en|black||said of a character or symbol filled with color}}.
#: ''Compare two Unicode symbols: {{l|mul||☞}} = "'''WHITE''' RIGHT POINTING INDEX"; {{l|mul||☛}} = "BLACK RIGHT POINTING INDEX"''
# Characterised by the presence of [[snow]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''white''' Christmas}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''white''' Easter}}
# {{lb|en|of a set of armor}} [[alwhite|Alwhite]], pertaining to {{w|white armor}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2015-08-31|author=Albrecht Classen|title=Handbook of Medieval Culture|publisher=Walter de Gruyter GmbH & Co KG|isbn=9783110392920
|text=For instance, tyro jousters in fifteenth-century Iberia wore “'''white''' armor,” while experienced men wore surcoats over their harness (Fallows 2010, 80), while fabric-covered breastplates are depicted in many examples of fifteenth-century northern European art.}}

